	abstract = {Fractured bones can require complicated restorative procedures due to the need for specialized biomaterials that can temporarily provide the necessary mechanical support while the body continues to heal biologically. Prefabricated scaffolds serve as essential supportive frameworks for engineers constructing bone tissues as they simulate the human extracellular matrix and aid the body in the construction of new tissue. This study focuses on the impact of scaffold structure on its mechanical properties by examining scaffold porosity using finite element analysis (FEA), in which a comparative analysis of porous as well as solid titanium alloy scaffolds was conducted. While previous studies have examined the trade-off between scaffold porosity and mechanical strength, the novelty of this study is demonstrated by showing that a scaffold with 18% porosity provides improved load distribution and lower maximum strain compared to a solid scaffold, by challenging the common assumption that introducing porosity weakens structural performance. In ANSYS, potential scaffold design models were created and all models underwent identical loading scenarios. To examine how the presence of pores affects stress concentration, the deformation of the scaffold in terms of shape and structure, along with the Von Mises stress and strain, were the variables that were mainly observed when compared to the scaffold without pores it showed the highest stress value. This occurred because stress become concentrated near the edges containing pore at the point where the strut meets. Still the scaffold with pores showed the load distribution was uniform and these findings highlight the balance that must be achieved between the strength of the scaffold in bearing loads and its ability to interact effectively with living tissuesThe results demonstrate that even moderate porosity can enhance structural efficiency. This works offers quantitative evidence linking pore architecture to mechanical performance and biological compatibility, providing practical guide for scaffold design. © 2026 IEEE.},
