	abstract = {The conventional strategy of replacing entire aerospace titanium alloy components with minor damage is economically inefficient and poses potential compatibility risks. Therefore, developing efficient and precise repair technologies is crucial for enhancing the lifecycle reliability and cost-effectiveness of critical equipment. In this study, a near-α high-temperature titanium alloy, Ti65, was pre-stretched to induce controlled plastic damage. The effects of electro-pulsing treatment (EPT) with varying pulse durations at a consistent current density on the microstructure evolution, defect healing behavior, and mechanical properties of the pre-damaged Ti65 alloy were systematically investigated. The results indicate that EPT effectively regulates the α(α')/β phase fraction and promotes dislocation rearrangement, substructure formation, and texture evolution through the synergistic effect of the Joule heating effect and electron wind force. Significantly, EPT directly causes crack tip closure, which operates in synergy with the interface migration induced by the α/β phase transformation to reduce the defect size and density. Fracture behavior analysis further confirmed that the crack morphology and quantity, along with high intragranular dislocation density, had a detrimental impact on plasticity during tensile deformation. Notably, the EPT70 sample exhibited a substantial enhancement in plasticity, attributable to effective defect healing, multiscale microstructure formation, and reduced dislocation density. The EPT70 specimen exhibited a yield strength of 1059.7 MPa and an ultimate tensile strength of 1256.1 MPa. Its elongation after fracture was 15.17%, a 7.59% increase compared to the as-received Ti65 alloy. Consequently, this study elucidates the microscopic mechanisms of plastic damage repair and plasticity enhancement in the Ti65 alloy via EPT processing, providing a novel technical approach and theoretical basis for the efficient and short-duration in-service repair of critical aerospace titanium alloy components. © 2026 Published by Elsevier B.V.},
